{"chapters":[{"num":1,"title":"Event Loop & Runtime","intro":"Almost every senior Node.js interview starts here, because the event loop explains performance, latency spikes and most \"why did this run first?\" bugs. Interviewers want more than \"Node is single-threaded\": they want you to name the phases, explain nextTick versus promises versus setImmediate, predict output of tricky snippets, and know how libuv's thread pool and the OS share the work. A senior answer always connects the mechanics to production: what blocks the loop, how you measure it, and how you fix it.","anims":14,"questions":[{"id":"1.1","title":"Is Node.js single-threaded?","level":"Core","tags":["event loop","libuv","threads"]},{"id":"1.2","title":"Node architecture: V8 vs libuv","level":"Core","tags":["architecture","v8","libuv"]},{"id":"1.3","title":"What is the event loop?","level":"Core","tags":["event loop","call stack","queues"]},{"id":"1.4","title":"The event loop phases","level":"Core","tags":["event loop","phases","libuv"]},{"id":"1.5","title":"nextTick vs Promise microtasks","level":"Core","tags":["microtasks","process.nextTick","promises"]},{"id":"1.6","title":"setTimeout(0) vs setImmediate","level":"Senior","tags":["timers","setImmediate","event loop"]},{"id":"1.7","title":"Puzzle: timers and microtasks","level":"Senior","tags":["puzzle","microtasks","timers"]},{"id":"1.8","title":"async/await under the hood","level":"Senior","tags":["async/await","promises","microtasks","puzzle"]},{"id":"1.9","title":"Puzzle: inside an I/O callback","level":"Deep dive","tags":["puzzle","setImmediate","microtasks","I/O"]},{"id":"1.10","title":"libuv thread pool","level":"Senior","tags":["libuv","thread pool","UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE","performance"]},{"id":"1.11","title":"Blocking the event loop","level":"Senior","tags":["performance","event loop lag","perf_hooks","profiling"]},{"id":"1.12","title":"Fixing CPU-heavy work","level":"Senior","tags":["performance","worker_threads","setImmediate","streams"]},{"id":"1.13","title":"nextTick and microtask starvation","level":"Deep dive","tags":["process.nextTick","microtasks","starvation"]},{"id":"1.14","title":"Process lifecycle, ref and unref","level":"Deep dive","tags":["lifecycle","unref","process exit","event loop"]}]},{"num":2,"title":"Async JavaScript & Error Handling","intro":"Almost every Node bug that reaches production is an async bug: a promise nobody awaited, an error thrown where no catch can see it, a loop that did not wait, or a fan-out that flattened a database. Interviewers use these questions to see whether you can predict what runs when and where an error goes. A senior answer explains the mechanism (promises, microtasks, the event loop), names the failure mode, and shows the production fix: limits, timeouts, cancellation, retries and a clear crash policy.","anims":14,"questions":[{"id":"2.1","title":"Callbacks to Promises to async/await","level":"Core","tags":["callbacks","promises","async/await","util.promisify"]},{"id":"2.2","title":"Promise states and chaining","level":"Core","tags":["promises","then","async functions"]},{"id":"2.3","title":"Promise.all vs allSettled vs race vs any","level":"Core","tags":["promises","Promise.all","combinators"]},{"id":"2.4","title":"Sequential vs parallel awaits","level":"Core","tags":["async/await","performance","Promise.all"]},{"id":"2.5","title":"The forEach + async pitfall","level":"Core","tags":["async/await","arrays","bugs"]},{"id":"2.6","title":"Limiting concurrency","level":"Senior","tags":["concurrency","promises","backpressure"]},{"id":"2.7","title":"Where errors go in async code","level":"Core","tags":["error handling","try/catch","timers"]},{"id":"2.8","title":"unhandledRejection and uncaughtException","level":"Senior","tags":["error handling","process","reliability"]},{"id":"2.9","title":"Floating promises and fire-and-forget","level":"Senior","tags":["promises","reliability","error handling"]},{"id":"2.10","title":"Timeouts and cancellation","level":"Senior","tags":["AbortController","AbortSignal","timeouts","timers/promises"]},{"id":"2.11","title":"Async iterators, for await, events.once/on","level":"Senior","tags":["async iterators","for await","events","streams"]},{"id":"2.12","title":"Error objects, cause, and error types","level":"Senior","tags":["errors","custom errors","error.cause"]},{"id":"2.13","title":"Retry with exponential backoff","level":"Senior","tags":["retry","backoff","resilience"]},{"id":"2.14","title":"Thenables and the microtask cost of await","level":"Deep dive","tags":["microtasks","await","thenables","V8"]}]},{"num":3,"title":"Streams & Buffers","intro":"Streams are how Node.js handles large or never-ending data with small, steady memory use, and Buffers are the raw bytes underneath. Interviewers ask about them because they separate people who have shipped file uploads, exports and proxies under load from people who have not. A senior answer talks about backpressure, error handling and cleanup with pipeline, and the memory cost of each choice.","anims":12,"questions":[{"id":"3.1","title":"What streams are and why","level":"Core","tags":["streams","memory"]},{"id":"3.2","title":"The four stream types","level":"Core","tags":["streams","readable","writable","duplex","transform"]},{"id":"3.3","title":"Flowing vs paused mode","level":"Core","tags":["streams","readable"]},{"id":"3.4","title":"Backpressure and drain","level":"Senior","tags":["streams","backpressure","highWaterMark"]},{"id":"3.5","title":"pipe vs pipeline","level":"Senior","tags":["streams","pipeline","errors"]},{"id":"3.6","title":"Writing a Transform stream","level":"Senior","tags":["streams","transform","coding"]},{"id":"3.7","title":"Object mode","level":"Core","tags":["streams","objectMode"]},{"id":"3.8","title":"Huge file line by line","level":"Core","tags":["streams","readline","async iteration"]},{"id":"3.9","title":"Streaming HTTP downloads and uploads","level":"Senior","tags":["streams","http","upload","download"]},{"id":"3.10","title":"Buffer basics","level":"Core","tags":["buffer","encoding","memory"]},{"id":"3.11","title":"Readable.from and async generators","level":"Senior","tags":["streams","async generators","async iteration"]},{"id":"3.12","title":"Common stream bugs","level":"Deep dive","tags":["streams","debugging","errors"]}]},{"num":4,"title":"Memory & Performance","intro":"Memory leaks, GC pauses and a blocked event loop are the most common reasons a Node.js service falls over in production. Interviewers ask about them to see whether you can reason about what V8 is doing and debug with real tools instead of guessing. A senior answer explains the mechanism in plain words, names the exact flags and APIs, and always starts with measuring.","anims":12,"questions":[{"id":"4.1","title":"Stack vs heap, V8 heap layout","level":"Core","tags":["memory","v8","heap"]},{"id":"4.2","title":"How garbage collection works","level":"Senior","tags":["memory","gc","v8"]},{"id":"4.3","title":"--max-old-space-size and containers","level":"Senior","tags":["memory","containers","v8 flags"]},{"id":"4.4","title":"process.memoryUsage() fields","level":"Core","tags":["memory","monitoring"]},{"id":"4.5","title":"Common memory leak sources","level":"Senior","tags":["memory","leaks"]},{"id":"4.6","title":"Debugging a leak in production","level":"Deep dive","tags":["memory","leaks","heap snapshot","debugging"]},{"id":"4.7","title":"WeakMap and WeakRef for caches","level":"Senior","tags":["memory","WeakMap","WeakRef","cache"]},{"id":"4.8","title":"In-process LRU cache with limits","level":"Senior","tags":["cache","memory","LRU","coding"]},{"id":"4.9","title":"CPU profiling and flame graphs","level":"Senior","tags":["performance","cpu profile","flame graph"]},{"id":"4.10","title":"Finding a slow API endpoint","level":"Senior","tags":["performance","latency","debugging"]},{"id":"4.11","title":"Moving CPU work to worker_threads","level":"Senior","tags":["performance","worker_threads","cpu"]},{"id":"4.12","title":"Cost of JSON.parse / JSON.stringify","level":"Senior","tags":["performance","JSON","event loop"]},{"id":"4.13","title":"perf_hooks and event loop utilization","level":"Deep dive","tags":["performance","perf_hooks","event loop","ELU"]}]},{"num":5,"title":"Core Modules & the Process","intro":"Senior interviews check that you know the building blocks under every framework: EventEmitter, fs, http, process, and the ways to use more than one CPU core. A strong answer explains not only the API but what blocks the event loop, what keeps the process alive, and how it behaves during deploys and failures.","anims":9,"questions":[{"id":"5.1","title":"How EventEmitter works","level":"Core","tags":["events","EventEmitter"]},{"id":"5.2","title":"'error' event, once and leaks","level":"Senior","tags":["events","errors","memory leaks"]},{"id":"5.3","title":"fs: sync, callback, promises","level":"Core","tags":["fs","thread pool","blocking"]},{"id":"5.4","title":"path, __dirname, import.meta","level":"Core","tags":["path","ESM","CommonJS"]},{"id":"5.5","title":"The http module and streams","level":"Core","tags":["http","streams"]},{"id":"5.6","title":"process: env, argv, exit codes","level":"Core","tags":["process","exit codes"]},{"id":"5.7","title":"Graceful shutdown","level":"Senior","tags":["signals","shutdown","production"]},{"id":"5.8","title":"The cluster module","level":"Senior","tags":["cluster","scaling","multi-core"]},{"id":"5.9","title":"worker_threads","level":"Senior","tags":["worker_threads","CPU","concurrency"]},{"id":"5.10","title":"child_process: spawn, exec, execFile, fork","level":"Senior","tags":["child_process","security"]},{"id":"5.11","title":"cluster vs worker_threads vs child_process","level":"Senior","tags":["cluster","worker_threads","child_process","comparison"]},{"id":"5.12","title":"crypto basics","level":"Core","tags":["crypto","security"]},{"id":"5.13","title":"The os module","level":"Core","tags":["os","operations"]},{"id":"5.14","title":"Timers: ref and unref","level":"Senior","tags":["timers","event loop","shutdown"]}]},{"num":6,"title":"Modules & Packages","intro":"Module bugs are some of the most confusing in Node: a value that is undefined only at startup, a package that works with import but not require, or a build that differs between laptops and CI. Interviewers ask about modules and npm to see if you understand how code is actually loaded and how you keep the dependency tree reproducible and safe.","anims":6,"questions":[{"id":"6.1","title":"CommonJS vs ES modules","level":"Core","tags":["modules","ESM","CommonJS"]},{"id":"6.2","title":"How require() works","level":"Senior","tags":["CommonJS","require","module cache"]},{"id":"6.3","title":"Circular dependencies","level":"Deep dive","tags":["CommonJS","ESM","circular"]},{"id":"6.4","title":"\"type\", .mjs/.cjs and \"exports\"","level":"Senior","tags":["package.json","ESM","exports"]},{"id":"6.5","title":"ESM and CommonJS interop","level":"Deep dive","tags":["ESM","CommonJS","interop","require(esm)"]},{"id":"6.6","title":"package.json fields that matter","level":"Core","tags":["package.json","npm"]},{"id":"6.7","title":"package-lock.json and npm ci","level":"Core","tags":["npm","lockfile","CI"]},{"id":"6.8","title":"Semver: ^ vs ~","level":"Core","tags":["semver","npm"]},{"id":"6.9","title":"dependencies, devDependencies, peerDependencies","level":"Core","tags":["npm","peerDependencies"]},{"id":"6.10","title":"npx and npm exec","level":"Core","tags":["npm","npx","CLI"]},{"id":"6.11","title":"Dependency security","level":"Senior","tags":["security","npm audit","supply chain"]}]},{"num":7,"title":"Express & APIs","intro":"Most Node backend jobs involve building HTTP APIs, usually with Express or Fastify. Interviewers use these questions to see if you understand what the framework actually does with a request: middleware order, how errors travel, what changed in Express 5, and how to make an API behave predictably with correct status codes, validation, limits and logs. A senior answer explains the mechanism and then the production gotcha.","anims":7,"questions":[{"id":"7.1","title":"How middleware works","level":"Core","tags":["express","middleware","next"]},{"id":"7.2","title":"Router and modular routes","level":"Core","tags":["express","router","structure"]},{"id":"7.3","title":"Error middleware and 404s","level":"Core","tags":["express","errors","404"]},{"id":"7.4","title":"Async errors: Express 4 vs 5","level":"Senior","tags":["express","async","errors","express 5"]},{"id":"7.5","title":"Request lifecycle and \"headers already sent\"","level":"Senior","tags":["express","http","ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT"]},{"id":"7.6","title":"Request validation","level":"Core","tags":["express","validation","zod","errors"]},{"id":"7.7","title":"HTTP status codes done right","level":"Core","tags":["http","status codes","api"]},{"id":"7.8","title":"Idempotency, PUT vs PATCH","level":"Core","tags":["rest","http","idempotency"]},{"id":"7.9","title":"Pagination: offset vs cursor","level":"Senior","tags":["api","pagination","sql"]},{"id":"7.10","title":"Body limits and file uploads","level":"Senior","tags":["express","uploads","multer","streams","limits"]},{"id":"7.11","title":"CORS in Express","level":"Senior","tags":["express","cors","http","browser"]},{"id":"7.12","title":"Structured logging with request IDs","level":"Senior","tags":["logging","pino","observability","AsyncLocalStorage"]},{"id":"7.13","title":"Express vs Fastify","level":"Deep dive","tags":["fastify","express","performance","schemas"]}]},{"num":8,"title":"Databases from Node","intro":"Almost every Node backend talks to a database, and most production incidents involve it: exhausted pools, slow queries, double inserts, lost updates. Interviewers want to see that you know what happens between your await and the database — connections, transactions, locks and error codes — and that you push correctness into the database instead of hoping JavaScript checks are enough. Expect practical code questions with pg, mysql2, Prisma or Mongoose.","anims":6,"questions":[{"id":"8.1","title":"Connection pooling","level":"Core","tags":["database","pool","postgres","performance"]},{"id":"8.2","title":"SQL injection and parameterized queries","level":"Core","tags":["sql","security","pg","mysql2"]},{"id":"8.3","title":"Transactions in code","level":"Core","tags":["transactions","postgres","pg","pool"]},{"id":"8.4","title":"The N+1 query problem","level":"Senior","tags":["orm","performance","prisma","sequelize","mongoose"]},{"id":"8.5","title":"ORM vs query builder vs raw SQL","level":"Senior","tags":["orm","prisma","knex","kysely","sql"]},{"id":"8.6","title":"Indexes and EXPLAIN","level":"Senior","tags":["sql","indexes","explain","performance"]},{"id":"8.7","title":"Race conditions in app code","level":"Senior","tags":["concurrency","race condition","locking","sql"]},{"id":"8.8","title":"Optimistic locking with a version column","level":"Senior","tags":["concurrency","optimistic locking","sql","mongoose"]},{"id":"8.9","title":"Handling DB errors and retries","level":"Senior","tags":["errors","retries","postgres","mysql","prisma"]},{"id":"8.10","title":"MongoDB and Mongoose essentials","level":"Core","tags":["mongodb","mongoose","indexes","lean"]},{"id":"8.11","title":"Schema migrations","level":"Senior","tags":["migrations","deployment","prisma","knex"]}]},{"num":9,"title":"Auth & Security","intro":"Security questions test whether you can be trusted with user data. Interviewers expect you to know how to store passwords, how sessions and tokens really work, and the common web attacks — XSS, CSRF, injection, SSRF, prototype pollution — together with the concrete Node code that prevents them. A senior answer names the threat first, then the defense, then the trade-off or the gap that remains.","anims":7,"questions":[{"id":"9.1","title":"Hashing passwords","level":"Core","tags":["passwords","bcrypt","argon2","crypto"]},{"id":"9.2","title":"Sessions vs JWT","level":"Core","tags":["auth","sessions","jwt","cookies"]},{"id":"9.3","title":"JWT structure and verification","level":"Senior","tags":["jwt","auth","crypto"]},{"id":"9.4","title":"Access tokens, refresh tokens, storage","level":"Senior","tags":["jwt","refresh tokens","cookies","localStorage"]},{"id":"9.5","title":"OAuth2 / OIDC login with PKCE","level":"Senior","tags":["oauth2","oidc","pkce","sso"]},{"id":"9.6","title":"CORS: what it protects","level":"Core","tags":["cors","browser","security"]},{"id":"9.7","title":"CSRF and SameSite cookies","level":"Senior","tags":["csrf","cookies","samesite","security"]},{"id":"9.8","title":"XSS, output encoding and Helmet","level":"Core","tags":["xss","csp","helmet","headers"]},{"id":"9.9","title":"Injection and input validation","level":"Core","tags":["injection","nosql","command injection","validation"]},{"id":"9.10","title":"Prototype pollution","level":"Deep dive","tags":["prototype pollution","javascript","security"]},{"id":"9.11","title":"Rate limiting and brute-force protection","level":"Senior","tags":["rate limiting","brute force","token bucket","redis"]},{"id":"9.12","title":"Secrets and dependency risks","level":"Senior","tags":["secrets","env","npm","supply chain"]},{"id":"9.13","title":"SSRF basics","level":"Senior","tags":["ssrf","fetch","security","webhooks"]}]},{"num":10,"title":"Testing & TypeScript","intro":"Senior engineers are expected to ship code that is safe to change. Interviewers ask about testing to see whether you can get real confidence quickly, without slow, flaky or over-mocked suites, and about TypeScript to see whether you use the type system to prevent bugs while remembering that types vanish at runtime.","anims":6,"questions":[{"id":"10.1","title":"Unit vs integration vs e2e","level":"Core","tags":["testing","strategy"]},{"id":"10.2","title":"Jest, Vitest and node:test","level":"Core","tags":["testing","Jest","Vitest","node:test"]},{"id":"10.3","title":"Mocking modules and HTTP","level":"Senior","tags":["mocking","nock","msw","testing"]},{"id":"10.4","title":"Fake timers","level":"Senior","tags":["testing","timers","mocking"]},{"id":"10.5","title":"Testing an Express API with supertest","level":"Core","tags":["supertest","Express","integration tests"]},{"id":"10.6","title":"Testing async code and rejections","level":"Core","tags":["testing","async","promises"]},{"id":"10.7","title":"Test database strategies","level":"Senior","tags":["testing","database","testcontainers"]},{"id":"10.8","title":"TypeScript: strict, type vs interface, unknown","level":"Core","tags":["TypeScript","tsconfig"]},{"id":"10.9","title":"Generics and typing Express","level":"Senior","tags":["TypeScript","generics","Express"]},{"id":"10.10","title":"Running TypeScript in Node","level":"Senior","tags":["TypeScript","tsc","tsx","type stripping"]},{"id":"10.11","title":"Runtime validation with Zod","level":"Senior","tags":["TypeScript","Zod","validation"]}]},{"num":11,"title":"Hands-on Coding Questions","intro":"Senior Node.js interviews almost always include a \"write it from scratch\" round: Promise.all, a concurrency pool, an EventEmitter, debounce, an LRU cache, a rate limiter. Interviewers are not testing memory of library code; they watch how you handle ordering, errors, edge cases, timers and memory. A senior answer states the approach first, writes clean code that handles the empty and failure cases, gives the complexity, and names the production trade-offs (cancellation, per-process state, unbounded growth).","anims":12,"questions":[{"id":"11.1","title":"Implement Promise.all","level":"Core","tags":["promises","async","coding"]},{"id":"11.2","title":"Concurrency-limited promise pool","level":"Senior","tags":["promises","concurrency","coding"]},{"id":"11.3","title":"Implement an EventEmitter","level":"Core","tags":["events","coding","classes"]},{"id":"11.4","title":"Implement debounce","level":"Core","tags":["timers","closures","coding"]},{"id":"11.5","title":"Implement throttle","level":"Core","tags":["timers","closures","coding"]},{"id":"11.6","title":"Retry with exponential backoff and jitter","level":"Senior","tags":["resilience","promises","coding"]},{"id":"11.7","title":"Implement promisify","level":"Core","tags":["promises","callbacks","coding"]},{"id":"11.8","title":"LRU cache with O(1) get and set","level":"Senior","tags":["data structures","caching","coding"]},{"id":"11.9","title":"Token-bucket rate limiter middleware","level":"Senior","tags":["express","rate limiting","coding"]},{"id":"11.10","title":"Count lines and words in a huge file","level":"Senior","tags":["streams","fs","memory","coding"]},{"id":"11.11","title":"TTL cache and async memoize","level":"Senior","tags":["caching","promises","coding"]},{"id":"11.12","title":"withTimeout using AbortController","level":"Senior","tags":["promises","AbortController","timers","coding"]}]}]}